  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$13.67B** of aluminum and aluminum products in 2025, down 5.2% from 2024 and 6% below the 2022 peak of $14.51B. A third of it (**33%**, $4.46B) was aluminum scrap (7602) — used beverage cans, wheels, turnings and other recyclable aluminum — shipped mostly to Thailand, India, Malaysia, Hong Kong and South Korea. Most of the rest is sheet and plate (7606, $2.55B), other articles (7616), ingot and billet (7601) and extrusions (7604), much of it for **Mexico and Canada**, which together took **50%**. Exports have jumped in 2026: **$11.47B** from January to August, up 25%.\n\nScrap enters most Asian buyers duty-free, so there the buyer's permit, inspection and purity rules decide access. **China** is the exception: U.S. scrap pays **35%** there (a 25% counter-tariff from 2018 plus the 10% extra duty on all U.S. goods). For finished aluminum, **Canada** has surtaxed U.S. ingot, extrusions, sheet, foil and most articles since March 13, 2025, at **50% since September 8, 2026**, even when they qualify for CUSMA; scrap and cans are not covered. **Mexico** takes USMCA-qualifying aluminum free but charges a US$1.72/kg anti-dumping duty on U.S. hollow profiles, and the **EU** cut its duty on U.S. aluminum to 0% from July 1, 2026.",
